  "body": "Closes the three gaps found in the earlier rotation audit. 4.16.0 -> 4.17.0 (MINOR).\n\n## 1. The limiter and the rotation engine did not know about each other\n\nVerified before the fix:\n\n```\nlimiter.is_blocked()      -> True     (60s reactive block)\nrotation state of key 0   -> HEALTHY\nrotation still selects    -> 0\n```\n\nSo with 3 keys and key 0 throttled, round-robin still sent a third of traffic to it, and those requests **waited inside `wait_if_blocked()`** while idle keys went unused. `acquire()` now takes an `avoid` set.\n\n**Fallback matters:** if every credential is unavailable, selection proceeds normally rather than returning -1. Refusing outright would turn a soft guardrail into a self-inflicted outage.\n\n## 2. Backoff ignored what the provider actually said\n\nEvery 429 waited a hardcoded 60s. Now parsed from the upstream headers:\n\n```\nretry-after: 5            -> 5.0\nretry-after-ms: 2500      -> 2.5\nx-ratelimit-reset: 6m0s   -> 360.0\nx-ratelimit-reset: 250ms  -> 0.25\nno/garbage headers        -> 60.0   (unchanged fallback)\nretry-after: 999999       -> 3600.0 (capped)\n```\n\n`retry-after-ms` is read first since it is the most precise.\n\n## 3. Daily quotas had no representation\n\nA daily cap resets at a **fixed wall-clock instant**, not N seconds after a failure — structurally inexpressible as a cooldown. `DailyQuotaTracker` counts per credential per *provider-local* day, with `{ENV}_DAILY_LIMIT` and `{ENV}_QUOTA_RESET_UTC_OFFSET` (Gemini = -8).\n\nIn-memory by design: the tracker avoids wasting requests rather than enforcing billing, and persisting would add a write to every request path. Documented in both the README and `.env.example`.\n\n## Also\n\nAn unrecognised `{ENV}_ROTATION` value now warns instead of silently pinning traffic to key 0 — previously a typo like `round-robin` disabled rotation with zero signal.\n\n## Design note\n\n`throttle_remaining()` is a `BaseProvider` method returning 0.0, overridden by the two providers that actually hold a limiter. I first tried an optional `_rate_limiter` on the base, which weakened the type at 7 existing call sites; overriding keeps those sites non-optional.\n\n## Verification\n\n- 831 provider tests pass, including 15 new header tests, 8 quota tests, and rotation tests covering skip, fallback-when-all-unavailable, and budget exhaustion.\n- One test I wrote initially failed and the **code was right** — with `limit=1` across 2 keys, two requests exhaust both and the fallback correctly engages. Test corrected, and the fallback now has its own explicit test.\n- v4.17.0 tag and wheel published so the installer contract tests resolve.",
