    "message": "feat(rotation): route around throttled and out-of-budget credentials\n\nThree gaps meant a healthy-looking credential could still be the wrong one to\npick.\n\nThe rate limiter and the rotation engine did not know about each other. A\ncredential blocked by its own limiter still reported HEALTHY, so rotation\nselected it and the request sat inside wait_if_blocked() while an idle\ncredential went unused. ``acquire`` now takes the set of credentials that\ncannot serve this instant and skips them, falling back to normal selection\nwhen every credential is in that state -- a soft guardrail must not become a\nself-inflicted outage.\n\nRate-limit backoff was a hardcoded 60 seconds. Providers publish the real\nreset on every 429, so it is parsed from Retry-After, retry-after-ms, and the\nX-RateLimit-Reset-* family, including the suffixed forms (``6m0s``, ``250ms``)\nand HTTP-date, capped at an hour and falling back to a minute when a provider\nsays nothing.\n\nDaily budgets had no representation at all. A daily cap resets at a fixed\nwall-clock instant -- Groq at UTC midnight, Gemini at Pacific -- which a\ncooldown timer cannot express, so DailyQuotaTracker counts per credential per\nprovider-local day and rotation prefers credentials with budget left.\n{ENV}_DAILY_LIMIT sets the budget, {ENV}_QUOTA_RESET_UTC_OFFSET moves the\nboundary. Counts are in-memory: the tracker exists to avoid wasting requests,\nnot to enforce billing, and persisting it would add a write to every request.\n\nAn unrecognised {ENV}_ROTATION value now logs a warning instead of silently\npinning every request to the first key.\n\nAdmin key health gains throttle_remaining and the daily budget fields.",
